'' A FIXED-LENGTH STRING GLOBAL IS A BYTE IMAGE, and its padding is not the same in
'' all four flavours. Measured:
''
''   zstring * 8 = "abc"        char Z$[8] = "abc"
''   wstring * 8 = wstr("abc")  uint16 W$[8] = { L'a', L'b', L'c' }
''   ustring * 8 = "abc"        uint16 U$[8] = "abc"
''   string  * 8 = "abc"        uint8 S$[8] = "abc     "
''
'' -- the three terminated flavours ZERO-fill and 'string * N' SPACE-fills, which is
'' what makes it the odd one out here as well as in its length tag.
''
'' AND THEY TRUNCATE DIFFERENTLY. Measured at N = 4 with a seven-character source:
'' 'zstring * 4' keeps "abc", 'string * 4' keeps "abcd" and 'wstring * 4' keeps three
'' units -- N-1 usable characters against N, the same capacity difference the length
'' tag records.
''
'' 'wstr( "abc" )' is a literal too where a wide destination is concerned: fbc folds it
'' to the same image a bare literal gives, and it is how every wide fixed-length
'' global in the corpus is written.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/fixstr-global.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered
'' EXPECT-OUT: z size 8 = bytes 61 62 63 00 00 00 00 00
'' EXPECT-OUT: s size 8 = bytes 61 62 63 20 20 20 20 20
'' The wide image IS the target: 'wstring * 8' is 16 bytes of 2-byte units on
'' win64 and 32 bytes of 4-byte ones on linux-x86_64. Both are asserted; the
'' per-target goldens carry the rest of the IR.
'' EXPECT-OUT[win64]: w size 16 = bytes 61 00 62 00 63 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00
'' EXPECT-OUT[linux-x86_64]: w size 32 = bytes 61 00 62 00 63 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00
'' EXPECT-OUT: s2 size 4 = bytes 61 62 63 64
'' GOLDEN: expected/fixstr-global.out
